BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:d0c976e5503f2f6c@delhi-events
DTSTAMP:20260920T125633Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:In the Moonlit Kingdom
LOCATION:KNMA\, New Delhi\, Kiran Nadar Museum of Art\, Saket\, New Delhi
  110017
URL:https://www.knma.org/whats-on/events/in-the-moonlit-kingdom
DESCRIPTION:KNMA presents Penguin Dialogues: In the Moonlit Kingdom \, fe
 aturing award-winning author Aishwarya Jha in conversation with Ambreen K
 han . Centred on the launch of Jha’s second novel\, the discussion expl
 ores a Gothic world shaped by an ancient manor house\, the family that in
 habits it\, and the secrets and spectres that linger between generations.
  The conversation delves into the novel’s themes of complex family dyna
 mics\, female rage\, memory\, and belonging\, while examining how Gothic 
 fiction can illuminate the tensions that both bind and fracture relations
 hips. It also explores the process of world-building\, focusing on the fi
 ctional Himalayan town at the heart of the novel and the lost Shangrilas 
 that fiction can bring back to life. The evening concludes with an audien
 ce Q&A and book signing\, offering readers an opportunity to engage direc
 tly with the author and continue the conversation beyond the stage. About
  the Speakers Aishwarya Jha is an award-winning author\, playwright\, and
  actress. Her debut novel\, The Scent of Fallen Stars \, won the 2024 Ram
  Nath Goenka Sahithya Samman for Fiction. Her writing has appeared in pub
 lications including Bombay Literary Magazine \, Scroll \, and The New Ind
 ian Express \, and she is the co-founder of Indophile\, an initiative ded
 icated to promoting Indian culture and heritage. Ambreen Khan is Head of 
 Corporate Communications at The Indian Express and an Adjunct Professor o
 f Media Studies at O.P. Jindal Global University and Jamia Millia Islamia
 . Her work spans journalism\, education\, cultural advocacy\, and strateg
 ic communications\, with a particular interest in storytelling and the in
 tersection of culture and public engagement.\n\nhttps://www.knma.org/what
 s-on/events/in-the-moonlit-kingdom
CATEGORIES:talk\,literature
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:0d469fb1c0d7a618@delhi-events
DTSTAMP:20260920T125633Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260926
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260927
SUMMARY:The Language of Belonging
LOCATION:KNMA\, New Delhi\, Kiran Nadar Museum of Art\, Saket\, New Delhi
  110017
URL:https://www.knma.org/whats-on/events/the-language-of-belonging
DESCRIPTION:As part of KNMA’s celebration of International Week of Deaf
  People\, this panel brings together diverse Deaf perspectives to explore
  the relationship between sign language\, Deaf culture\, communication\, 
 identity\, and belonging. The conversation considers how these themes int
 ersect with art and creative spaces\, and how museums and cultural instit
 utions can foster greater inclusion and participation for Deaf and hearin
 g communities alike. Moderated by Hardeep Singh \, the panel brings toget
 her Sunil Sahasrabudhe \, Ranveet Kau r\, Harshali Nade and Niharika Popl
 i . Together\, they share perspectives on Deaf culture\, language\, acces
 sibility\, and ways of creating spaces that support meaningful communicat
 ion and participation. Following the panel\, a screening of If I Could Te
 ll You \, a documentary by Niharika Popli \, explores the experiences of 
 Deaf individuals through themes of language\, identity\, communication\, 
 and the human need to express and be understood. Following Charu\, a youn
 g Deaf woman\, alongside a hearing CODA (Child of Deaf Adults) and four y
 oung artists\, the film considers different forms of visual and performat
 ive expression. About the Contributors Hardeep Singh is a Deaf artist\, d
 esigner\, and inclusion professional at KNMA\, working to advance accessi
 bility and Deaf inclusion in museum spaces. Sunil Sahasrabudhe is Preside
 nt of the All India Federation of the Deaf (AIFD) and a leader\, educator
 \, and advocate for the Deaf community in India. Ranveet Kaur is a Deaf c
 ommunity leader\, Coordinator at the Speaking Hands Institute for the Dea
 f\, and President of The Punjab Association of Deaf. Harshali Nade is an 
 Indian Sign Language translator and communication professional with exper
 ience across news\, museums\, media\, and accessibility. She works to mak
 e information more accessible to the Deaf community through ISL translati
 on. Niharika Popli is a filmmaker\, editor\, and co-founder of Dilmaya Fi
 lms. Her work explores language\, identity\, and belonging. Her debut doc
 umentary\, Rasan Piya\, received the Tareque Masud Award at Film Southasi
 a and a Special Mention at the Mumbai International Film Festival.\n\nhtt
 ps://www.knma.org/whats-on/events/the-language-of-belonging
CATEGORIES:talk\,art\,cinema\,sociology
END:VEVENT
